✅ Problem Statement
Goal: Implement two stacks in a single array.
You must design two stacks that share the same array space but have separate operations (i.e., independent push and pop operations for both stacks).

💡 Key Idea
The idea is to use two pointers or indices:
One pointer for the first stack starting from the left (left_index).
One pointer for the second stack starting from the right (right_index).
The first stack grows from the left side of the array.
The second stack grows from the right side of the array.
They will move towards each other, and as long as the left pointer doesn't surpass the right pointer, we can perform operations for both stacks.

📘 Algorithm
Initialize an empty array of size n.
Set left_index = 0 and right_index = n - 1.
For Stack 1:
Push operation: Increase left_index, place the value at array[left_index].
Pop operation: Decrease left_index.

For Stack 2:
Push operation: Decrease right_index, place the value at array[right_index].
Pop operation: Increase right_index.
Operations:
Push Stack 1: array[left_index++] = value
Pop Stack 1: array[left_index--]
Push Stack 2: array[right_index--] = value
Pop Stack 2: array[right_index++]

🧑‍💻 Python Program
class TwoStacks:
    def __init__(self, size):
        self.size = size
        self.stack = [None] * size  # Single array to hold two stacks
        self.left_index = -1  # Start of first stack
        self.right_index = size  # Start of second stack

    # Push in stack 1
    def push_stack1(self, value):
        if self.left_index + 1 == self.right_index:
            print("Stack Overflow: No space available")
            return
        self.left_index += 1
        self.stack[self.left_index] = value

    # Pop from stack 1
    def pop_stack1(self):
        if self.left_index == -1:
            print("Stack Underflow: Stack 1 is empty")
            return
        value = self.stack[self.left_index]
        self.left_index -= 1
        return value

    # Push in stack 2
    def push_stack2(self, value):
        if self.right_index - 1 == self.left_index:
            print("Stack Overflow: No space available")
            return
        self.right_index -= 1
        self.stack[self.right_index] = value

    # Pop from stack 2
    def pop_stack2(self):
        if self.right_index == self.size:
            print("Stack Underflow: Stack 2 is empty")
            return
        value = self.stack[self.right_index]
        self.right_index += 1
        return value

# Example usage
ts = TwoStacks(10)

# Operations on stack 1
ts.push_stack1(10)
ts.push_stack1(20)
ts.push_stack1(30)
print("Pop from stack 1:", ts.pop_stack1())  # Output: 30

# Operations on stack 2
ts.push_stack2(40)
ts.push_stack2(50)
ts.push_stack2(60)
print("Pop from stack 2:", ts.pop_stack2())  # Output: 60

⏱ Time and Space Complexity
Metric	Value
Time Complexity	O(1) for each push and pop operation (constant time)
Space Complexity	O(n) (the array size is n, so the space used by the stacks is proportional to the array size)

🔍 Example Walkthrough
For size = 10:
stack1 starts at index 0 and grows towards the right (left_index increases).
stack2 starts at index 9 and grows towards the left (right_index decreases).

Steps:
Push 10 to stack1 → stack[0] = 10
Push 20 to stack1 → stack[1] = 20
Push 30 to stack1 → stack[2] = 30
Pop from stack1 → return 30, decrement left_index.

For stack2:
Push 40 to stack2 → stack[9] = 40
Push 50 to stack2 → stack[8] = 50
Pop from stack2 → return 50, increment right_index
